description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs lines 982-1038. verify_smime_signer (982-1005) and verify_smime_recipient (1015-1038) have IDENTICAL function bodies:

    let policy = profile.policy(now_unix);
    let validated = verify_chain(
        chain,
        anchors,
        &policy,
        &DefaultVerifier,
        revocation,
        &NoAiaFetcher,
    )?;
    pkix_identity::verify_mailbox(&chain[0], mailbox)?;
    Ok(validated)

The rustdoc on verify_smime_recipient (lines 1007-1014) acknowledges this:

    Identical mechanics to verify_smime_signer; see that function's rustdoc...
    The two wrappers differ only in name so callers can communicate signer-vs-recipient intent at the call site.

The signer-vs-recipient distinction is encoded entirely in the caller-supplied Profile (the profile decides whether to require digitalSignature or keyEncipherment in the leaf's KeyUsage). The function name is a marker visible at the call site, not a code-path difference.

SUGGESTED REWRITE: pick one. Either:

(a) Single verify_smime_mailbox(chain, anchors, mailbox, profile, now, revocation) that's role-agnostic; let the caller pick the profile to communicate intent. The intent IS in the Profile already; the wrapper name is a redundant signal.

(b) Keep both names but factor the body:

    pub fn verify_smime_signer(...) -> Result<...> {
        verify_smime_role(chain, anchors, mailbox, profile, now_unix, revocation)
    }
    pub fn verify_smime_recipient(...) -> Result<...> {
        verify_smime_role(chain, anchors, mailbox, profile, now_unix, revocation)
    }
    fn verify_smime_role(...) -> Result<...> { /* the shared body */ }

(b) preserves the call-site readability and avoids the duplicate body. (a) is cleaner but is a public API change (one fewer exported function).

NOT URGENT — the duplication is small (5 lines) and the docstring is honest about it. Flag for the next refactor pass. The same pattern doesn't apply to verify_tls_server vs verify_tls_client_dns because those take DIFFERENT identity types (ServerName vs Option<ServerName>) and the body uses verify_dns_name unconditionally vs only when Some, so the bodies actually differ.
